In terms of color patterns and markings, accordions can vary widely. Some accordions have a natural wood finish, while others may be painted in bright colors or feature intricate designs. The buttons and keys are typically white or black, providing contrast against the body of the instrument.

The size and proportions of accordions also differ. The accordion in image 1 appears to be a standard concertina-style accordion, which is relatively small and compact. The accordion in image 2 is slightly larger, suggesting it might be a diatonic button accordion, commonly used in folk music. The accordion in image 3 is even larger, indicating it could be a chromatic button accordion, often associated with jazz and classical music.

Behaviorally, accordions are played by pressing the buttons or keys on the top panel while manipulating the bellows with the hands. This requires coordination between the fingers and the bellows to produce the desired notes and rhythms.

